  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nBroad upper level ridging continues to promote warm and dry \nweather over the northern Plains this afternoon, with mostly \nclear to partly cloudy skies and light (around 10-15 MPH) south \nto southeast winds expected through the afternoon. With the \nensemble consistently underperforming with high temperatures \nthis week we have nudged temperatures a 1 to 3 degrees higher \nthis afternoon utilizing a 1:1 blend of NBM:NBM90, resulting in \nforecast highs broadly in the 70s this afternoon. Have opted to \nleave forecast low temperatures in the mid 40s to mid 50s as \nincreasing cloud cover this evening and overnight will prevent \nthe low temperature crash we've seen earlier this week. A few \nclimate sites are set to break their daily record high min, \nincluding BIS, DIK, JMS, and XWA.\n\nConditions on Friday will then be characterized by an upper \nlevel trough lifting across the Rockies and eventually closing \noff over the southern Canadian Prairies through the early \nweekend. With flow over North Dakota turning southerly as the \ntrough approaches, Friday is expected to the warmest day of the \nweek with highs from the lower 70s to lower 80s. During the \ndaytime lee cyclogenesis is expected to spit out a center of low\npressure over southern Montana, which is then to slowly be \nlifted into the forecast area through the evening and overnight \ninto Saturday. The passage of this low and its attendant cold \nfront is expected to promote chances (20 to 50 percent) for rain\nover portions of the west. With the warmer weather we have been\nmonitoring the potential for isolated thunderstorms \naccompanying these showers early Friday evening, but with \nsurface winds turning southwesterly and advecting drier air \nacross the west Friday afternoon the chances remain very low \n(less than 10 percent).\n\nThe main forecast challenge for Friday and Saturday will be the\npotential for strong winds across much of western and central \nNorth Dakota. Increasing low level lapse rates and an initial \npush of CAA will allow gusty south to southwest winds (sustained\n20 to 25 MPH gusting up to 35 MPH) to mix down from a develop \n850mb over portions of the west. Later Friday night through \nSaturday morning, stronger CAA and greater pressure rises is \nthen expected to promote strong northwesterly winds sustained \naround 25 to 30 MPH and gusting as high as 45 MPH across the \narea. Have continued Portions of the forecast area are expected \nto approach Wind Advisory criteria during this period, though \nconfidence is too low for headlines at this time. \n\nOtherwise for Saturday and beyond, cooler conditions with highs\nbroadly in the 40s and 50s are expected behind the cold front. \nLooking ahead, there's decent ensemble agreement on swinging \nanother closed low out of the south across western and central \nNorth Dakota early Monday through Tuesday, and again Wednesday \nthrough early Thursday. The NBM advertises near daily low to \nmedium chances for PoPs through this period, with enough \nvariation in storm track between ensemble members to limit \nconfidence at this time.",
